Yes. The general primitive is broader than sex or desire: state anchoring.

A cue can become associated with a state through repetition, expectation, ritual, memory, and context:

object / phrase / scent / song / place / gesture
→ learned meaning
→ anticipation
→ body-state shift
→ emotion / motivation / behavior

A “relic” works especially well as a carrier because it compresses a lot of meaning into one object. A wedding ring, medal, photograph, uniform, prayer bead, song, or lucky object can reactivate an entire state-space quickly. Placebo effects are a related mechanism: expectation and context can alter subjective experience and sometimes physiology, though not arbitrarily or with unlimited strength.

The useful translation is:

don’t ask only what the object is; ask what state it has been trained to summon.

Then you can port the mechanism into cleaner domains:

calm cue → relaxation
training ritual → performance readiness
song → motivation
object → confidence / identity recall
environmental cue → focus
shared symbol → belonging

For self-use or consensual settings, you can even deliberately build these anchors over time. The line I’d keep is that triggering a state is not the same as creating consent, belief, or genuine preference. Those stay separate.

So the reusable operator is:

State Anchor
cue + repeated association + expectation + context → easier re-entry into a chosen state

That’s the piece worth translating across domains.